            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you regarding the above-named 70-year-old male patient admitted on 15th December 2004 with a one-day history of right iliac fossa pain, nausea, and low-grade fever, clinically consistent with acute appendicitis. On admission, physical examination, blood tests, and abdominal ultrasound confirmed the diagnosis. The patient was adequately resuscitated with intravenous fluids, commenced on intravenous antibiotics, and prepared for theatre. A laparoscopic appendectomy was performed on 16th December 2004 without intraoperative complications. Postoperatively, wound care was maintained, vital signs monitored closely, and analgesia managed effectively. The patient recovered well and was mobilised with physiotherapy support. He was discharged home on 19th December 2004 in good general condition. Please arrange follow-up in approximately two weeks. Do not hesitate to contact me should any concerns arise.</div>"
            },
